Current Projects

LabREC — Research and Development for Civil Registration
LabREC is a research and development initiative focused on technologies for Brazil’s civil registration ecosystem. Its activities combine academic research with the design and evaluation of technological solutions for digital trust, identity, electronic documents, and artificial intelligence.
Research and development topics include:

digital identity and authentication;
digital signatures and long-term validation;
electronic documents and preservation;
public key infrastructures;
verifiable credentials;
artificial intelligence applied to civil registration;
privacy and information security;
trustworthy digital infrastructures.

Several technological initiatives have emerged from this environment, including ICP-RC, IdRC, and AI-based systems for the civil registration ecosystem.


Post-Quantum Cryptography
This research investigates cryptographic mechanisms and infrastructures capable of remaining secure in the presence of quantum computers.
Current interests include:

post-quantum public-key cryptography;
hybrid classical/post-quantum protocols;
post-quantum digital signatures;
post-quantum certificates and PKI;
quantum-safe voting systems;
migration strategies for existing digital infrastructures.

A particular concern is the practical transition from today’s cryptographic infrastructures to quantum-resistant systems.


Finite-Field Arithmetic
This research investigates efficient arithmetic over binary finite fields, particularly polynomial-basis representations of \(\mathrm{GF}(2^m)\).
Topics include:

irreducible polynomials;
structured families of pentanomials;
modular reduction;
multiplication algorithms;
XOR complexity;
circuit depth;
computational search for efficient polynomial families;
AI-assisted discovery of algebraic structures.

The objective is to identify mathematical structures that lead to efficient implementations of cryptographic arithmetic.


AI Agents, Identity, and Digital Trust
Artificial intelligence agents increasingly perform actions that have consequences outside the computational environment in which they operate.
This research studies mechanisms for establishing trust in such agents, including:

agent identity;
authentication;
authorization;
delegation;
accountability;
auditability;
non-repudiation;
verifiable actions and evidence.

A broader research question is how traditional digital trust infrastructures must evolve when autonomous AI agents become active participants in digital ecosystems.

Selected Research & Development Projects

Digital Signatures and Long-Term Electronic Documents
A long-standing research line investigates the use of digital signatures for electronic documents and the preservation of their evidentiary value over long periods.
Research topics have included:

digital signature formats;
long-term signatures;
signature validation;
document preservation;
public key infrastructures;
cryptographic evidence;
trusted services;
location of modifications in signed data.



Digital Identity and Public Key Infrastructure
Research on digital identity has evolved from traditional PKI-based architectures toward federated identity, self-sovereign identity, verifiable credentials, and emerging identity models for humans, organizations, devices, and software agents.


Electronic Voting
Research in electronic voting has explored cryptographic mechanisms for verifiability, privacy, and trustworthy election processes.
This line includes work on verifiable voting protocols and, more recently, the incorporation of post-quantum cryptography into electronic voting architectures.


Hash-Based Digital Signatures
Research on hash-based signatures has investigated efficient encodings and variants of the Winternitz signature family, including techniques for improving performance and reducing signature size.


Cryptographic Protocols and Security Infrastructures
Several projects have addressed the design, implementation, and analysis of cryptographic protocols and security infrastructures.
The emphasis is not only on cryptographic primitives, but also on their integration into operational systems involving identities, keys, authorization, audit, and governance.

